<section class="block-section do">
  <span class="section-tag">Do</span>
  <h2>Today's work: everyone does this</h2>
  <div class="callout factstorm">
    <div class="callout-title">Factstorm: four minutes</div>
    <p>Groups of three or four, one recorder. The question: how many different ways does Earth move material from one place to another? Name the force, and a real place you've seen it work; real examples count double. Everything goes on the list; we judge after, not during. Harvest to the board. Today's four agents should already be hiding in your lists; the lesson is finding them there.</p>
  </div>
  <p>Keep using the printed note guide from Block 1. Part 2 is erosion.</p>
  <div class="callout task">
    <div class="callout-title">The task: One place, one agent</div>
    <p>With a partner, find one place in or near Chennai in Google Earth where material is being moved. Take a screenshot. Name the agent (water, wind, ice or gravity), say where the material came from, and say where it is going.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="callout support">
    <div class="callout-title">If you want support</div>
    <p><a href="https://www.ck12.org/book/ck-12-earth-science-for-high-school/section/10.1/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Easier reading: CK-12 Chapter 10.1, Water Erosion and Deposition</a>. A gentler version of the erosion ideas in Earle 5.3.</p>
    <p>A sentence frame: "At ___, ___ is moving ___ from ___ to ___. I can tell because ___."</p>
  </div>
  <div class="callout extend">
    <div class="callout-title">If you want to go further</div>
    <p>Compare two agents at the same place and say which one moves more material in a year, and why.</p>
  </div>
